FINAL REPORT — TRP1
Architecting the AI-Native IDE & Intent–Code Traceability

1. Executive Summary
This project transforms Roo Code into a governed, intent-driven AI-native IDE through a
deterministic hook-based middleware layer. The system enforces a mandatory Intent →
Context → Action → Trace protocol and establishes cryptographically verifiable semantic
traceability between declared business intents and source code mutations.
Traditional Git systems track textual diffs but are blind to semantic intent and AST-level identity.
This gap introduces trust debt, context rot, and governance failures in agentic workflows. The
delivered solution addresses these challenges by:
● Enforcing a strict two-stage agent handshake (intent selection before mutation)
● Intercepting every tool invocation through a composable Hook Engine
● Dynamically injecting structured intent context from a sidecar orchestration database
● Maintaining an append-only, AI-native trace ledger with cryptographic content hashing
for spatial independence
● Implementing optimistic concurrency control to enable safe parallel agent execution
● Persisting cross-session rules and lessons in a shared project brain (CLAUDE.md)
Result: every AI-generated code mutation is verifiably attributable to a specific business intent,
producing a governed, auditable, and parallel-safe software engineering environment.

2. System Overview
2.1 Goal
Upgrade Roo Code from a conversational coding assistant into a governed agentic IDE that
enforces the disciplined flow:
Intent → Context → Action → Trace
This design prevents unauthenticated, out-of-scope, or conflicting mutations and enables
reliable scaling of multi-agent software development.

3. Architecture
3.1 High-Level Architecture
(Diagram referenced below)
The system introduces a middleware governance layer between the agent and all
mutation-capable tools. This layer is deterministic, composable, and enforced at runtime, not by
convention.

4. Hook Middleware Design
4.1 Hook Engine
A clean, composable interceptor that wraps all tool executions (HookEngine, TypeScript).
Key properties:
● Deterministic — identical inputs yield identical outcomes
● Isolated — hooks are ordered, independent, and may short-circuit execution
● Fail-safe — explicit, recoverable errors instead of silent corruption
● Extensible — governance rules are added as new Hook implementations

4.2 Pre-Hooks
IntentPreHook
Intercepts select_active_intent(intent_id):
● Validates intent ID against .orchestration/active_intents.yaml
● Injects a structured <intent_context> block (XML)
● Blocks execution if no valid intent is selected
ScopeHook
On write_file:
● Verifies file paths against the active intent’s owned_scope (via minimatch)

Example rejection:
Scope violation: src/auth/middleware.ts not owned by INT-001

StaleHook (Optimistic Locking)
On write_file:
● Computes SHA-256 of current disk content when baseHash is provided
● Rejects writes with STALE_FILE if content has diverged
● Forces re-read, enabling safe parallel work

4.3 Post-Hooks
TraceHook
After a successful write_file:
● Computes SHA-256 of new content
● Builds a structured trace entry linking:
○ intent ID
○ file path
○ line range
○ content hash
○ contributor metadata (AI / “roo”)
● Appends the record to .orchestration/agent_trace.jsonl (append-only)

5. AI-Native Git Layer
5.1 Agent Trace Ledger — .orchestration/agent_trace.jsonl
An append-only, line-delimited JSON ledger. Each mutation record ensures spatial
independence via cryptographic hashing; file moves or line shifts do not invalidate attribution.

Example entry:
{
"id": "uuid-v4",
"timestamp": "2026-02-18T14:30:22Z",
"files": [
{
"relative_path": "src/weather/service.ts",
"conversations": [
{
"contributor": {
"entity_type": "AI",
"model_identifier": "roo"
},
"ranges": [
{
"start_line": 1,
"end_line": 42,
"content_hash": "sha256:a8f5f167f44f4964e6c998dee827110c..."
}
],
"related": [
{
"type": "intent",
"value": "INT-001"
}
]
}
]
}
]
}

This ledger creates verifiable Intent ↔ AST ↔ Code traceability.

6. Intent Orchestration Database — .orchestration/
A machine-managed sidecar directory:
● active_intents.yaml — lifecycle, scope, constraints, acceptance criteria
● agent_trace.jsonl — semantic mutation ledger
● intent_map.md — human-readable intent-to-file mapping
● CLAUDE.md — shared brain (rules, lessons learned, cross-agent memory)

7. Intent-Driven Agent Protocol
A mandatory two-stage handshake enforced via system prompt:
You are an Intent-Driven Architect AI inside a governed IDE.
RULES:
1. You MUST NOT write or modify files before selecting an intent.
2. First analyze the request.
3. Call select_active_intent(intent_id).
4. Wait for <intent_context> injection.
5. Then execute scoped actions.
If no valid intent → STOP and explain.

8. Parallel Agent Orchestration
Parallelism is enabled safely through:
● Scope partitioning — intents own disjoint file globs
● Optimistic locking — prevents blind overwrites
● Shared brain (CLAUDE.md) — accumulates rules and lessons across sessions
● Append-only trace ledger — conflict-free attribution merging
Multiple agents can operate concurrently across chat panels without semantic collisions.

